---
import CategoryLayout from '@/layouts/CategoryLayout.astro';
import { SLIPPER_SPECS } from '@/data/specs';
import { categoryTitle } from '@/lib/seo';

const title = categoryTitle('Slippers', ['Waffle', 'Coral Fleece']);
---

<CategoryLayout
  title={title}
  description="Disposable hotel slippers wholesale in terry cloth, waffle, coral fleece and dense velvet. Five SKUs, custom logo on request. MOQ 50 pcs."
  h1="Hotel slippers"
  breadcrumb="Hotel Slippers"
  specs={SLIPPER_SPECS}
  images={['slipper', 'slipper2']}
  specLead="Four faces, five SKUs. Sole construction varies by SKU; tell us the bathroom floor and we will narrow it down."
>
  <p>
    Slippers are the cheapest thing on a hotel's textile order and the one guests mention most.
    That mismatch is why the fabric choice matters more than the price per pair.
  </p>
  <p>
    We make five SKUs across four faces: terry cloth, waffle, coral fleece and dense velvet.
    Which one a property picks usually comes down to whether the slipper is meant to be thrown
    away or taken home, and what the bathroom floor is made of.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">The four faces</h2>
  <p>
    Terry cloth is the standard hotel slipper. Loop pile, soft, absorbs a wet foot, and reads as
    the conventional choice. It is also the slowest of the four to dry.
  </p>
  <p>
    Waffle is the open weave. It dries noticeably faster than terry and packs flat, which is why
    it turns up in spa and resort programmes. The texture shows wear sooner, so it suits a
    shorter replacement cycle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Coral fleece is the warm one. Soft pile, a bit more substantial underfoot, and the face
    guests tend to keep. For a cold-climate property, this is often the upsell.
  </p>
  <p>
    Dense velvet has a flat, tight face and behaves closest to a reusable slipper. It is the
    build for suites, not for standard rooms.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Soles and floors</h2>
  <p>
    A marble bathroom floor in a wet climate is not the same risk as dry carpet in a desert
    hotel, and the sole should differ. Some of our SKUs are built for grip, others for comfort.
    Tell us the floor surface and the climate at inquiry and we will point at the right SKUs
    rather than send the full list.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Disposable or not</h2>
  <p>
    There is no industry definition of disposable. A guest decides that by whether the slipper
    survives the first night. Terry and waffle are the ones guests usually leave behind. Coral
    fleece and dense velvet are the ones they tend to pack. That is good branding or an
    unplanned cost, depending on the property.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the plan is to launder and reuse, say so. That is a different construction and it needs
    to be quoted as one.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Branding</h2>
  <p>
    Logo embroidery and woven labels are both possible on these SKUs. Send the artwork and the
    placement you want, and confirm the colour count, because a multi-colour embroidered logo
    changes both the price and the minimum.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Packing</h2>
  <p>
    Slippers ship in individual polybags, in pairs, or in a printed carton with the amenity set
    if the property uses one. Packing style affects carton cube and therefore freight, so it
    belongs in the quote rather than in a follow-up email.
  </p>
</CategoryLayout>
